    "content": "Great context \u2014 doubling users and data volume across two new markets within 18 months, plus a 30% headcount increase, is exactly the kind of trajectory our architecture is designed to absorb. Let me address the migration question directly.\n\n**How the API-first layer handles legacy migration:**\n\n- **Phased, parallel-run approach**: Rather than a big-bang cutover, we typically migrate in waves (e.g., by region or business unit) while your legacy systems stay live. This is the key to avoiding operational disruption \u2014 your teams keep working in the old system until each wave is validated in the new one.\n- **Staged ingestion via APIs**: Data is pulled from legacy systems through our integration layer, mapped to the target schema, and validated before commit. Because the model is extensible, we map your legacy fields to custom objects/fields without forcing you to abandon your existing data structures.\n- **Validation and reconciliation**: Each wave includes record-count reconciliation and data-quality checks so you can confirm integrity before decommissioning any legacy source.\n- **Incremental sync during transition**: For the parallel-run period, delta syncs keep both systems aligned so nothing falls through the cracks.\n\n**On typical timeline and effort \u2014 I want to be careful here:**\nMigration timelines vary significantly based on the number and complexity of legacy systems, data quality, and how much transformation is needed. I don't want to quote you a number that doesn't reflect your actual environment. What I can say is that the effort is typically front-loaded into discovery and mapping, then the actual migration waves move faster once the mapping is proven.\n\nTo give you a credible estimate, I'd need to understand:\n- How many legacy systems are involved, and what they are\n- Roughly how much data (records, history depth)\n- Whether there are regulatory/data-residency requirements for the two new markets (this can affect architecture)\n\nRather than guess, I'd suggest we fold this into the follow-up with our solutions architect \u2014 they can scope a realistic migration plan against your specifics. Would you like me to set up that session and note these migration questions as the agenda focus?\n\nI'll also log this as a strategic opportunity signal in the CRM so the team has full context. Shall I proceed with scheduling the follow-up?",
